---
/*  What Reverb adds to the window every plugin shares: its room, its send and return, and the reflections it
    draws for the probe. The pictures are Reverb's own, drawn by its picture tool. */
import TutorialPage from '../../layouts/TutorialPage.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const img = (name: string) => `${base}/tutorial/${name}.webp`;
const window = `${base}/tutorial/the-window`;

const parts: [string, string][] = [
  ['room', 'the room'],
  ['send-return', 'send and return'],
  ['scene', 'the scene'],
  ['try', 'things to try'],
];
---

<TutorialPage
  id="reverb"
  sub="one room around the whole field, reflections and tail"
  parts={parts}
  window={{ src: img('reverb-window'), alt: 'A reverb window: the scene with the send and return regions and the reflections, the matrix, and the send tab.', caption: 'a reverb window' }}
>
  <div class="prose" slot="intro">
    <p class="lede">
      Reverb puts an ambisonic field in a room: early reflections from the room's walls, each arriving from its
      own direction, and a diffuse tail after them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its window is the one every bambi plugin has; the scene, the matrix, presets and the link are explained in
      <a href={window}>the window</a>. This page covers what is Reverb's own: its three tabs, <code>room</code>,
      <code>send</code> and <code>return</code>, and what it draws on the scene.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="room" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('reverb-room')} alt="The room tab: six rooms with hall chosen, size, decay, tone, roughness, distance, pre-delay, and the input's low and high cut." width="696" height="830" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>the room</h2>
      <p>
        The row at the top is six rooms to start from: <code>ambience</code>, <code>room</code>,
        <code>chamber</code>, <code>hall</code>, <code>large hall</code> and <code>cathedral</code>. Choosing one
        sets the room's numbers except pre-delay, and the input's cuts; it leaves pre-delay, the levels, the
        quality and the regions as they are. The room stays lit
        while you change its numbers, and a <strong>double-click</strong> brings them back.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>size</code></strong>, 3 to 40 metres: when the reflections arrive and how dense they are.</li>
        <li><strong><code>decay</code></strong>, 0.2 to 10 seconds: how long the room rings.</li>
        <li><strong><code>tone</code></strong>: how much faster the highs die than the lows.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>roughness</code></strong>: how much the walls scatter. Smooth walls give clear mirror
          reflections; rough ones send more into the tail.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>distance</code></strong>, 0.5 to 20 metres: how far away the sound is taken to be, heard
          mostly as how loud the dry is against the room. It is one value for everything that comes in, since a
          reverb on a bus cannot know each source's distance.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>pre-delay</code></strong>, up to 200 milliseconds: holds the tail back further, on top of
          where it already starts. The reflections stay where they are.
        </li>
        <li><strong>input</strong>: a <code>low cut</code> and a <code>high cut</code> on what goes into the room.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="send-return" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('reverb-send')} alt="The send tab: sectors chosen, four of them, their fill and softness." width="696" height="780" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>send and return</h2>
      <p>Reverb has two regions, each on its own tab:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>send</code></strong> is the part of the field that goes into the room.</li>
        <li><strong><code>return</code></strong> is where the room comes back.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Both are <code>everywhere</code> to start with. Narrow the send, and only what arrives from there is put
        in the room; narrow the return, and the room is heard only from there. The dry level still carries the
        whole field. Shaping and moving a region works the same in every plugin: see
        <a href={`${window}#regions`}>regions</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scene">
    <h2>the scene</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('reverb-scene')} alt="The scene with the probe placed: marks in Reverb's colour where the reflections arrive, inside the send and return regions." width="1236" height="602" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Place the <a href={`${window}#scene`}>probe</a> on the scene and Reverb answers with the room: a mark for
        each reflection a sound from there would get, where it arrives from and at its weight. Only the mirror
        reflections are drawn. What the walls scatter goes into the tail, which comes from everywhere and has no
        direction to draw. The answer is worked out from the room the plugin is in, so it changes as you change
        the room.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>things to try</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>A bigger room.</strong> Place the probe, choose <code>hall</code>, then <code>large hall</code>:
          the marks spread further apart and the tail grows longer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Clear walls, then rough ones.</strong> Turn <code>roughness</code> down and the reflections stand
          out as echoes off the walls; turn it up and they shrink as more of them goes into the tail.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A room behind you.</strong> In <code>return</code>, choose a spot and put it behind the
          listener. Whatever goes in, the room is heard from behind.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</TutorialPage>
